<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>LexiChunk Benchmark Report</title>
    <style>
        :root {
            --primary: #2563eb;
            --success: #16a34a;
            --warning: #d97706;
            --danger: #dc2626;
            --bg: #ffffff;
            --text: #1f2937;
            --border: #e5e7eb;
            --code-bg: #f3f4f6;
        }
        body {
            font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
            max-width: 1200px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 2rem;
            color: var(--text);
            line-height: 1.6;
        }
        h1 { color: var(--primary); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--primary); padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
        h2 { color: var(--primary); margin-top: 2rem; }
        h3 { color: #4b5563; }
        table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 1rem 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
        th { background: var(--primary); color: white; padding: 0.6rem; text-align: left; white-space: nowrap; }
        td { padding: 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
        tr:hover { background: #f9fafb; }
        .metric-good { color: var(--success); font-weight: bold; }
        .metric-bad { color: var(--danger); font-weight: bold; }
        .metric-ok { color: var(--warning); }
        .na { color: #9ca3af; }
        .summary-box {
            background: #eff6ff;
            border-left: 4px solid var(--primary);
            padding: 1rem 1.5rem;
            margin: 1rem 0;
            border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        }
        .warning-box {
            background: #fffbeb;
            border-left: 4px solid var(--warning);
            padding: 1rem 1.5rem;
            margin: 1rem 0;
            border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        }
        .superseded-box {
            background: #fef2f2;
            border-left: 4px solid var(--danger);
            padding: 1rem 1.5rem;
            margin: 1rem 0;
            border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
        }
        .chart-container { margin: 2rem 0; }
        .timestamp { color: #6b7280; font-size: 0.875rem; }
        .provenance { color: #6b7280; font-size: 0.9rem; }
        .badge {
            display: inline-block;
            padding: 0.25rem 0.5rem;
            border-radius: 4px;
            font-size: 0.75rem;
            font-weight: bold;
        }
        .badge-sig { background: #dcfce7; color: #166534; }
        .badge-nosig { background: #fee2e2; color: #991b1b; }
        .control-row { background: #eef2ff; }
        dt { margin-top: 1rem; }
        dd { margin-left: 1.5rem; color: #4b5563; }
        code { background: var(--code-bg); padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem; border-radius: 4px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>LexiChunk Benchmark Report</h1>
    <p class="timestamp">Generated: {{ timestamp }}</p>
    <p class="provenance">
        LexiChunk <code>{{ lexichunk_version }}</code> (commit <code>{{ lexichunk_commit }}</code>)
        &middot; seed <code>{{ seed }}</code>
        &middot; Strategies: {{ strategies | join(', ') }}
        &middot; Documents: {{ documents | length }}
        {% if models %}&middot; Embedding models: {{ models | join(', ') }}{% endif %}
    </p>

    <div class="summary-box">
        <strong>Summary:</strong> This report compares LexiChunk's legal document chunking
        against general-purpose alternatives across structural quality (scored against
        hand-checked gold annotations) and retrieval metrics.
    </div>

    <h2>Structural Quality Metrics (gold-scored)</h2>
    <div class="chart-container">
        {{ structural_chart_html | safe }}
    </div>

    <h3>Strategy x Document Heatmap</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        {{ structural_heatmap_html | safe }}
    </div>

    <h3>Detailed Structural Metrics (macro-averaged over n={{ n_structural_documents }} documents)</h3>
    {% if structural_rows %}
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Strategy</th>
                <th>Located</th>
                <th>Leaf Frag &darr;</th>
                <th>Over-merge &darr;</th>
                <th>Sub-clause Grp</th>
                <th>Head R</th>
                <th>Head P</th>
                <th>Def Attach &uarr;</th>
                <th>XRef R</th>
                <th>XRef P</th>
                <th>Size CV</th>
                <th>Avg Chars</th>
                <th>Chunks</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in structural_rows %}
            <tr>
                <td>{{ row.strategy }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.located }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.leaf_frag }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.over_merge }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.sub_clause_grp }}</td>
                <td class="{{ 'na' if row.head_r == 'n/a' }}">{{ row.head_r }}</td>
                <td class="{{ 'na' if row.head_p == 'n/a' }}">{{ row.head_p }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.def_attach }}</td>
                <td class="{{ 'na' if row.xref_r == 'n/a' }}">{{ row.xref_r }}</td>
                <td class="{{ 'na' if row.xref_p == 'n/a' }}">{{ row.xref_p }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.size_cv }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.avg_chars }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.chunks }}</td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    {% else %}
    <p><em>No structural metrics available.</em></p>
    {% endif %}

    {% if has_legacy_structural %}
    <h3>Legacy Structural Metrics — SUPERSEDED</h3>
    <div class="superseded-box">
        <strong>Do not cite as evidence about LexiChunk.</strong> These metrics derive their
        ground truth from LexiChunk's own parse of the document, so LexiChunk is graded
        against itself and scores well by construction, while the baselines' scores move
        whenever LexiChunk's parser changes even though the baselines have not. Retained
        for provenance only.
    </div>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Strategy</th>
                <th>Clause Frag.</th>
                <th>Def. Preserv.</th>
                <th>XRef Resol.</th>
                <th>Hierarchy</th>
                <th>Size CV</th>
                <th>Avg Chars</th>
                <th>Chunks</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in legacy_structural_rows %}
            <tr>
                <td>{{ row.strategy }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.clause_frag }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.def_preserv }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.xref_resol }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.hierarchy }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.size_cv }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.avg_chars }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.chunks }}</td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><em>n = {{ n_legacy_documents }} documents.</em></p>
    {% endif %}

    {% if has_retrieval %}
    <h2>Retrieval Quality Metrics</h2>
    <p>NDCG@10 is a compatibility field. Metric versions:
    {{ retrieval_metrics | map(attribute='ndcg_metric') | unique | join(', ') }}.
    Evidence-assignment scores are not classical chunk NDCG. Do not compare
    different versions or infer validated gains from unanchored annotations.</p>

    {% for model, chart_html in retrieval_charts.items() %}
    <h3>{{ model }}</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        {{ chart_html | safe }}
    </div>
    {% if retrieval_by_model[model] %}
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Strategy</th>
                <th>P@1</th>
                <th>P@3</th>
                <th>P@5</th>
                <th>P@10</th>
                <th>MRR</th>
                <th>NDCG@10</th>
                <th>DRM rate</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in retrieval_by_model[model].rows %}
            <tr>
                <td>{{ row.strategy }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p1 }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p3 }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p5 }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p10 }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.mrr }}</td>
                <td class="{{ 'metric-good' if row.ndcg10 | float > 0.8 else ('metric-bad' if row.ndcg10 | float < 0.4 else '') }}">
                    {{ row.ndcg10 }}
                </td>
                <td>{{ row.drm_rate }}</td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><em>n = {{ retrieval_by_model[model].n_queries }} queries.</em></p>
    {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}

    <h3>Embedding Model Comparison</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        {{ model_comparison_html | safe }}
    </div>

    <h3>Document Retrieval Mismatch</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        {{ drm_chart_html | safe }}
    </div>
    {% endif %}

    {% if has_comparisons %}
    <h2>Statistical Comparisons</h2>
    <div class="summary-box">
        The absolute delta and its bootstrap 95% CI are the headline figures — not a
        percentage improvement. A row is significant only if it survives Holm correction
        across the whole family of tests run in this comparison
        (see <code>p (Holm)</code> / <code>Holm sig</code>); the raw t-test and Wilcoxon
        p-values are shown for reference only. <code>rcts_1024</code> is the
        <strong>size-matched control</strong>.
    </div>
    {% for model, rows in comparisons_by_model.items() %}
    <h3>{{ model }}</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Metric</th>
                <th>A</th>
                <th>B</th>
                <th>n</th>
                <th>Mean A</th>
                <th>Mean B</th>
                <th>&Delta;</th>
                <th>95% CI</th>
                <th>p (t)</th>
                <th>p (Wilcoxon)</th>
                <th>p (Holm)</th>
                <th>Holm sig?</th>
                <th>Cohen's d</th>
                <th>LODO range</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in rows %}
            <tr class="{{ 'control-row' if row.b == 'rcts_1024' }}">
                <td>{{ row.metric }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.a }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.b }}{% if row.b == 'rcts_1024' %} <strong>(size-matched control)</strong>{% endif %}</td>
                <td>{{ row.n }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.mean_a }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.mean_b }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.delta }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.ci }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p_t }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p_wilcoxon }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p_holm }}</td>
                <td><span class="badge {{ 'badge-sig' if row.significant_holm else 'badge-nosig' }}">
                    {{ "YES" if row.significant_holm else "NO" }}
                </span></td>
                <td>{{ row.cohens_d }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.lodo }}</td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    {% endfor %}
    {% elif has_legacy_significance_fallback %}
    <h2>Statistical Significance</h2>
    <div class="warning-box">
        <strong>Warning:</strong> this run has no <code>ComparisonResult</code> data — showing
        single-test, uncorrected p-values with no bootstrap CI, no multiple-comparison
        correction, and no leave-one-document-out sensitivity.
    </div>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Metric</th>
                <th>Baseline</th>
                <th>Mean A</th>
                <th>Mean B</th>
                <th>p-value</th>
                <th>Significant?</th>
                <th>Effect Size (d)</th>
                <th>n</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for row in legacy_significance_rows %}
            <tr>
                <td>{{ row.metric }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.baseline }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.mean_a }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.mean_b }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.p_value }}</td>
                <td><span class="badge {{ 'badge-sig' if row.significant else 'badge-nosig' }}">
                    {{ "YES" if row.significant else "NO" }}
                </span></td>
                <td>{{ row.effect_size }}</td>
                <td>{{ row.n_queries }}</td>
            </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    {% endif %}

    <h2>Methodology</h2>
    <div class="summary-box">
        Ground truth for structural quality is hand-checked span annotations in
        <code>gold/</code> (see <code>GoldStructuralMetrics</code>) — not LexiChunk's own
        parse of the document. What these metrics do <strong>not</strong> measure: they say
        nothing about answer quality once a chunk is retrieved, nothing about latency or
        indexing cost, and nothing about documents or jurisdictions outside this fixture set.
        <code>heading_recall</code>/<code>heading_precision</code> and
        <code>xref_target_recall</code>/<code>xref_target_precision</code> are <code>n/a</code>
        (not <code>0</code>) for any strategy whose chunks expose no headings or
        cross-references — that is "not applicable," not "failed."
    </div>
    <h3>Metrics Definitions</h3>
    <dl>
        <dt><strong>Located</strong></dt>
        <dd>Fraction of a strategy's chunks that could be matched back to a contiguous span
        of the source document. A chunk that cannot be located is excluded from every other
        structural metric for that document.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Leaf Frag. (clause fragmentation rate)</strong></dt>
        <dd>Fraction of gold leaf clauses that are NOT &ge;80% contained inside a single
        chunk. Lower is better.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Over-merge (top-level over-merge rate)</strong></dt>
        <dd>Fraction of chunks that span more than one top-level gold clause. Lower is
        better.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Sub-clause Grp (sub-clause grouping rate)</strong></dt>
        <dd>Informational only — no "better" direction is asserted.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Head R / Head P (heading recall / precision)</strong></dt>
        <dd><code>n/a</code> for a strategy that exposes no headings in its chunk
        metadata.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Def Attach (definition attachment recall)</strong></dt>
        <dd>Fraction of defined-term uses whose definition is attached in the same or an
        adjacent chunk. Higher is better.</dd>

        <dt><strong>XRef R / XRef P (cross-reference target recall / precision)</strong></dt>
        <dd><code>n/a</code> for a strategy that emits no cross-references.</dd>

        <dt><strong>Precision@k / Recall@k</strong></dt>
        <dd>Fraction of top-k retrieved chunks that are relevant / fraction of all relevant
        chunks found in top-k, judged by span overlap with a gold relevant section — not by
        searching for its description text inside the chunk.</dd>

        <dt><strong>MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank)</strong></dt>
        <dd>1 / rank of the first relevant result, averaged across queries.</dd>

        <dt><strong>NDCG@10</strong></dt>
        <dd>Graded-relevance ranking metric (3=exact, 2=same section, 1=related). Formula:
        DCG@k / IDCG@k.</dd>

        <dt><strong>DRM rate (Document Retrieval Mismatch)</strong></dt>
        <dd>Fraction of top-k results from documents other than the query's target. With one
        shared index across all documents this is usually non-zero for every query, so the
        rate (not the boolean hit) is what carries information.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>Statistical Testing</h3>
    <p>Each comparison reports a paired bootstrap 95% CI on the mean delta, a paired t-test
    and Cohen's d, a Wilcoxon signed-rank test and matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation,
    and a leave-one-document-out (LODO) sensitivity range — recomputing the delta with each
    document's queries removed, to show how much the headline result depends on any single
    document. The Holm correction is applied across the whole family of tests run together
    (every metric, baseline, and embedding model), not per baseline.</p>

    <h3>References</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Anthropic. "Contextual Retrieval." 2024.</li>
        <li>Manning, Raghavan, Schutze. "Introduction to Information Retrieval." Cambridge UP.</li>
        <li>Jarvelin, Kekalainen. "Cumulated Gain-Based Evaluation of IR Techniques." ACM TOIS, 2002.</li>
        <li>Holm, S. "A Simple Sequentially Rejective Multiple Test Procedure." Scand. J. Statist., 1979.</li>
    </ul>

    <footer style="margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); color: #6b7280; font-size: 0.875rem;">
        Generated by sdk-scaffolder | LexiChunk Evaluation Harness
    </footer>
</body>
</html>
